        "q1": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What height do I need for a pool fence in North Carolina?",
            "answer": "<p>The North Carolina Residential Code, Appendix G, sets pool barriers at 48 inches. Our 46-inch panel installed in spigots gives a 48-inch railing, so it is the usual choice for North Carolina pool decks. Gates need gate hinges, a latch and, where the inspector asks for it, a self-closing setup. Confirm the details with your county before you order.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does salt air on the Outer Banks or in Wilmington affect the hardware?",
            "answer": "<p>Salt air is why the hardware is 2205 marine-grade stainless steel, with a 10-year no-rust promise. Tempered glass itself is not affected by salt. On an oceanfront deck in Nags Head or Wrightsville Beach, rinse the spigots and glass with fresh water now and then. Both finishes, Brushed Stainless and Matte Black, use the same 2205 base.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will the glass survive snow and freeze-thaw around Asheville and Boone?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Tempered 12 mm glass and stainless spigots do not absorb water, so freeze-thaw does not crack or swell them like wood pickets. Snow sliding off a roof onto a deck lands on the deck, not on the railing. In the mountains the deck framing carries the snow load; the railing only needs a sound attachment to the joists or a concrete slab.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need engineering documents for a coastal county permit?",
            "answer": "<p>Coastal counties in North Carolina have high design wind speeds, and the plan reviewer may ask how the railing is attached. We provide engineering documents with testing results and blocking details for top-mount and side-mount spigots on concrete and on wood. Download them from our engineering page and hand them to your building department with the permit application.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        }
