        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Where North Carolina installs glass railing</strong></p><p>Around Charlotte and Lake Norman, the typical projects are lake-house decks in Cornelius, Davidson and Mooresville. South Charlotte adds pool decks and stair guards in new builds. The Triangle is similar: Raleigh, Durham, Chapel Hill, Cary and Apex keep adding subdivisions where a glass deck railing replaces wood pickets.</p><p>In Asheville and the Blue Ridge, view decks face the ridgelines, and owners choose glass so the railing does not hide the mountains. Snow and freeze-thaw are normal there. Tempered glass and 2205 stainless steel handle both without paint or stain. On the coast, Wilmington, Wrightsville Beach and the Outer Banks combine salt air with tall beach houses on pilings. Greensboro, Winston-Salem and High Point round out the Piedmont with back decks, balconies and interior stair railings. For Mecklenburg County details, see <a href=\"/pages/north-carolina-glass-railing-charlotte\">glass railings in Charlotte</a>.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>North Carolina code in short</strong></p><p>North Carolina uses the 2018 North Carolina Residential Code and Building Code, based on the 2018 IRC and IBC. Both have been in force since January 1, 2019. The North Carolina code sets guards at 36 inches minimum for houses and 42 inches for commercial and multifamily buildings. For pools, the residential code's Appendix G sets a 48-inch barrier, which matches our 46-inch panel in spigots.</p><p>Coastal counties, including the Outer Banks and the Wilmington area, carry high design wind speeds. The local building department will look at how the spigots attach. Our engineering documents cover top-mount and side-mount spigots on concrete and on wood. Mountain counties add snow load to the deck itself, not to the railing. Every county and city reviews its own permits, so check with your local building department before ordering. The full summary is on our <a href=\"/pages/north-carolina-building-code-for-glass-railings\">North Carolina building code for glass railings</a> page.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Delivery anywhere in North Carolina</strong></p><p>Every order ships from our warehouse in Quebec and crosses the border before it reaches you. The price you see at checkout is final: no duties, tariffs or brokerage fees. Standard orders are processed in 2 to 3 business days. A freight carrier then delivers in 5 to 10 business days anywhere in the continental U.S. The truck has a liftgate, so the crate comes down to street level. That works at a lake house in Mooresville or a beach house in Nags Head. Custom glass needs 2 to 3 weeks of shipping after processing.</p><p>Orders over 3,000 USD ship free; details are on the <a href=\"/pages/free-shipping-information\">free shipping page</a>. Open the crate on arrival and report any damage within 3 days, before installation, and we replace or refund the item at no cost.</p>"
          }
        }
